<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chp-faq">
  <title>Frequently Asked Questions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This chapter collects questions that workshop participants commonly ask,
      organized by theme. If a question arises during the session that isn't
      here, it's a good candidate for the next revision of this document.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 1: Getting Started                        -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="faq-getting-started">
    <title>Getting Started</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-what-is-pretext">
      <title>What exactly is <pretext/>? Is it like <latex/> or like a CMS?</title>
      <p>
        <pretext/> is a semantic markup language written in XML. It is closer in
        spirit to <latex/> — you write a plain text source file and a build tool
        converts it to the output format you want — but the markup describes the
        <em>structure</em> of the content (definition, theorem, exercise) rather
        than its appearance. A CMS like WordPress or a word processor lets you
        click buttons to make text bold; <pretext/> asks you to tag text as
        <c>&lt;em&gt;</c> (emphasis) and then decides how to render that
        emphasis in each output format.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-need-latex">
      <title>Do I need to know <latex/> to use <pretext/>?</title>
      <p>
        Not for prose, lists, definitions, examples, or exercises — those use
        <pretext/>'s own tags and are easy to learn without any <latex/>
        background. You <em>do</em> need <latex/> syntax for mathematics, because
        <pretext/> uses <latex/> inside the <c>&lt;m&gt;</c> and <c>&lt;me&gt;</c>
        tags. If you can write <c>x^2 + 1</c> or <c>\frac{a}{b}</c>, you have
        enough. If you have never used <latex/> at all, a one-page cheat sheet
        of common math commands is usually sufficient to get started.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-need-xml">
      <title>Do I need to know XML?</title>
      <p>
        You need to follow two rules: every tag that opens must close
        (<c>&lt;p&gt;...&lt;/p&gt;</c>), and tags must nest properly. That's
        the practical extent of what most authors need. You do not need to
        understand XML schemas, namespaces, or DTDs to write or maintain a
        <pretext/> document.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-install">
      <title>What do I need to install to use <pretext/>?</title>
      <p>
        For today's workshop, nothing. <url href="https://pretext.plus">PreTeXt.Plus</url>
        runs entirely in your browser. For longer-term use, you can install the
        <pretext/> command-line interface with <c>pip install pretext</c>
        (Python 3.8+ required) or use a GitHub Codespace, which has everything
        preconfigured. A local install gives you more control over custom assets
        and multi-file projects.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-cost">
      <title>Is <pretext/> free?</title>
      <p>
        Yes. <pretext/> is open-source software released under the
        <url href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html">GNU General Public
        License</url>. There is no licensing cost for authoring, building, or
        publishing. PreTeXt.Plus offers a free tier with up to ten projects;
        hosting on GitHub Pages is also free. Runestone Academy charges for
        institution-level features (gradebooks, LMS integration) but is free for
        individual instructors and students.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 2: Writing and Authoring                  -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="faq-authoring">
    <title>Writing and Authoring</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-convert-latex">
      <title>Can I convert my existing <latex/> files to <pretext/>?</title>
      <p>
        There is a tool called <url href="https://github.com/bnmnetp/lxml2ptx">latexml</url>
        and a project called <url href="https://github.com/daverosoff/tex4ebook">tex4ebook</url>,
        but the most practical approach for most instructors is
        <url href="https://pandoc.org">Pandoc</url>, which can convert
        <c>.tex</c> to rough <pretext/> XML. The conversion will not be perfect —
        custom macros, complex tables, and TikZ figures require manual cleanup —
        but for a document that is mostly prose, definitions, and basic math, a
        Pandoc conversion followed by hand-editing is often faster than starting
        from scratch. The PreTeXt community discussion list has several threads on
        migration strategies.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-images">
      <title>How do I include images?</title>
      <p>
        Place the image file (PNG, JPG, or SVG) in your <c>assets</c> folder and
        reference it with <c>&lt;image source="filename.png"/&gt;</c>. For
        accessibility, add a <c>&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c> child element. You
        can also generate figures programmatically from <latex/> using TikZ or
        PGFPlots, in which case <pretext/> compiles the <latex/> code and outputs
        an SVG automatically — no external image file needed.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-exercises-graded">
      <title>Can exercises be auto-graded?</title>
      <p>
        Yes, when the document is hosted on Runestone Academy. True/false,
        multiple-choice, fill-in-the-blank, matching (card-sort), and
        clickable-area exercises are all auto-graded with per-answer feedback.
        Interactive coding exercises in Python, JavaScript, Java, C++, and
        several other languages also run in the browser and can have embedded
        unit tests. If you host on GitHub Pages instead of Runestone, the
        exercises are still displayed but are not graded or tracked.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-coauthoring">
      <title>Can multiple authors work on the same document?</title>
      <p>
        Yes. Because <pretext/> source files are plain text, they work
        naturally with <c>git</c> version control. Two authors can work on
        different chapters in separate files and merge their changes with the
        same tools used for software development. This is one advantage over
        word processors, where track-changes becomes unmanageable in large
        documents.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-numbering">
      <title>What controls numbering? Can I override it?</title>
      <p>
        <pretext/> numbers definitions, theorems, examples, exercises, and
        figures automatically in the order they appear in the source. The
        <c>publication.ptx</c> file controls the depth at which numbering
        resets (per chapter, per section, etc.). You can override an individual
        number only in unusual circumstances and it is generally discouraged —
        the strength of the system is that numbers are always consistent and
        cross-references never need to be updated manually.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 3: Accessibility                          -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="faq-accessibility">
    <title>Accessibility</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-a11y-screen-reader">
      <title>Is the HTML output usable with a screen reader?</title>
      <p>
        Yes. The HTML generated by <pretext/> uses a proper heading hierarchy,
        ARIA landmark roles, keyboard-accessible navigation, and MathJax to
        provide MathML alongside every rendered equation. Screen readers that
        support MathML — including JAWS with MathPlayer, NVDA with MathCAT, and
        VoiceOver on recent macOS/iOS — can read and structurally navigate
        equations. The output has been tested by the <pretext/> development team
        with multiple screen reader and browser combinations.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-a11y-wcag">
      <title>Is <pretext/> WCAG compliant?</title>
      <p>
        The standard HTML output targets WCAG 2.1 Level AA compliance.
        Structural features — headings, landmarks, keyboard focus, color
        contrast — are handled automatically by the <pretext/> stylesheets.
        Author-supplied content (images, video) also needs to meet the standard,
        which means every informative image requires a
        <c>&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c> and every video needs captions on the
        hosting platform. A document that is structurally correct and has
        complete image descriptions will meet AA. Perfect AAA compliance
        requires additional effort for some criteria (extended audio descriptions,
        sign language) and is not claimed by default.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-a11y-braille">
      <title>Can <pretext/> really produce Braille? How does that work?</title>
      <p>
        Yes. Running <c>pretext build braille</c> converts the source to a
        <c>.brf</c> Braille Ready Format file using the
        <url href="https://liblouis.io">liblouis</url> library for general text
        and Nemeth Code for mathematics. The output can be sent directly to a
        Braille embosser or opened in a Braille display emulator. This removes
        the need for a human Braille transcriber for standard mathematical
        content, which is often the biggest bottleneck in providing accessible
        STEM materials through disability services. Non-standard notation or
        highly complex diagrams may still require manual review.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-a11y-interactive">
      <title>What about GeoGebra and Desmos applets — are those accessible?</title>
      <p>
        GeoGebra and Desmos are visual tools and are not currently usable with a
        screen reader. For students who cannot interact with an applet, the best
        practice is to add a surrounding paragraph that describes what the applet
        illustrates and what the student should observe. This way the conceptual
        content is available in plain text even when the interactive is not. Some
        GeoGebra applets do provide limited keyboard navigation; check the
        specific applet. The <pretext/> native exercise types (true/false,
        multiple-choice, etc.) <em>are</em> fully keyboard-accessible.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-a11y-pdf">
      <title>Is the PDF accessible too?</title>
      <p>
        The PDF output is produced via <latex/> and is a high-quality typeset
        document, but PDF accessibility — tagged PDF with reading order, alt
        text attached to images, MathML embedded in equations — lags behind the
        HTML output. For screen reader users, the HTML version is strongly
        preferred. If a student specifically needs a tagged PDF (for example, to
        use with a PDF annotation tool), the <pretext/> team is working to improve
        this, and the ePub format is a better intermediate option in the meantime.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-a11y-dyslexia">
      <title>Are there options for students with dyslexia or reading disabilities?</title>
      <p>
        The <c>publication.ptx</c> file supports a <c>dyslexic-font</c> LaTeX
        style option for PDF output. For HTML, the CSS theming system allows
        fonts, line spacing, and letter spacing to be customized. Students can
        also use browser-level accessibility tools (font override, reader mode,
        text-to-speech extensions) on any <pretext/> HTML page without special
        configuration from the author.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 4: Publishing and Distribution            -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="faq-publishing">
    <title>Publishing and Distribution</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-hosting">
      <title>Where can I host a <pretext/> book?</title>
      <p>
        The most common options are:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>GitHub Pages</term> — free, automatic deployment when you
              push to GitHub, your book lives at a
              <c>username.github.io/reponame</c> URL. No server to maintain.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Runestone Academy</term> — free for instructors and students,
              adds a gradebook, LMS integration (Canvas, Blackboard), and
              student progress tracking. Requires a Runestone account for both
              instructor and students.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Your institution's web server</term> — upload the contents of
              the <c>output/web</c> folder to any standard web host.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>PreTeXt.Plus</term> — built-in hosting with a shareable link,
              no server required. Ideal for drafts and worksheets.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-lms">
      <title>Can I use <pretext/> materials inside my LMS (Canvas, Blackboard, etc.)?</title>
      <p>
        In two ways. First, you can link directly to the published HTML from your
        LMS. Second, if you host on Runestone Academy, you can integrate
        Runestone with your LMS via LTI, which allows grades from Runestone
        exercises to flow back into your LMS gradebook automatically. Direct
        embedding of <pretext/> HTML inside an LMS frame generally works but can
        have layout limitations depending on the LMS.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-copyright">
      <title>Who owns the content I write in <pretext/>?</title>
      <p>
        You do. <pretext/> is a tool; it does not claim any rights over content
        you produce with it. You can assign any license you like to your materials.
        Many <pretext/> authors choose a Creative Commons license (typically
        CC BY or CC BY-SA) to make their materials freely remixable, but this is
        entirely your choice. If your institution has an open educational resources
        policy, your IP office can advise on appropriate licensing.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-updates">
      <title>How do I update a published book?</title>
      <p>
        Edit the source, rebuild, and redeploy. On GitHub Pages, pushing a commit
        triggers an automatic rebuild via GitHub Actions. There is no separate
        CMS interface to log in to. Your published URL stays the same; readers
        who return to the link get the updated version automatically.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 5: Adoption and Long-Term Use             -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="faq-adoption">
    <title>Adoption and Long-Term Use</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-students-read">
      <title>Do students need to know anything about <pretext/> to read a book?</title>
      <p>
        No. Students see a polished website, a PDF, or an ePub — they never
        interact with the source XML. The reading experience is comparable to any
        other professionally typeset open textbook.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-longevity">
      <title>Will my content still be usable in ten years?</title>
      <p>
        This is one of the strongest arguments for <pretext/>. Your source is
        plain text XML, a format that will be readable by any text editor
        indefinitely. The conversion tools that turn that XML into HTML or PDF
        are open-source and maintained by an academic community with a long-term
        commitment to the format. Contrast this with a proprietary word processor
        format or a closed CMS, where format changes or discontinued products can
        strand your content. Several <pretext/> books that were written over a
        decade ago are still being maintained and updated today.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-getting-help">
      <title>Where do I go if I get stuck after the workshop?</title>
      <p>
        The best first stop is the
        <url href="https://pretextbook.org/community.html"><pretext/> community
        pages</url>, which link to the Google Groups discussion list and the
        Discord server. Both are active and welcoming to newcomers. The
        <url href="https://pretextbook.org/doc/guide/html/guide-toc.html"><pretext/>
        Author's Guide</url> is comprehensive. For quick syntax reminders, the
        cheatsheet in this hub covers the tags you'll reach for most often.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="faq-community">
      <title>Is there a community of <pretext/> users at MAA and similar organizations?</title>
      <p>
        Yes. <pretext/> has strong adoption among mathematicians and was
        originally developed by Rob Beezer at the University of Puget Sound for
        exactly the kind of content that MAA members write. There are active users
        at institutions of all sizes. The American Institute of Mathematics has
        supported the project financially and continues to host community
        infrastructure. At MAA meetings, <pretext/> sessions and minicourses
        appear regularly — this workshop is one example.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </section>

</chapter>
